## Findings

### #1 [CRITICAL] Hardcoded credentials / secrets (AP-01)
File: src/utils.js:2
File: src/utils.js:3
File: src/utils.js:4
File: src/utils.js:5
Description: The `config` object has literal secrets: `dbUser: "admin_master"`, `dbPass: "senha_super_secreta_prod_123"`, `paymentGatewayKey: "pk_live_1234567890abcdef"` (live key prefix `pk_live_`) and `smtpUser: "no-reply@fullcycle.com.br"`. The port is also fixed (`port: 3000`, line 6).
Impact: Anyone with access to the repo or its git history gets the production credentials. Secrets cannot be rotated or varied per environment.
Recommendation: Move them to a config module that reads environment variables, with a `.env.example` that has no real values, and keep defaults only for non-sensitive values (Playbook P-01).

### #2 [CRITICAL] God Class / God Method (AP-03)
File: src/AppManager.js:4-139
File: src/AppManager.js:25-138
Description: The `AppManager` class creates the connection (`new sqlite3.Database(':memory:')`, line 7), creates the schema and seeds it (`initDb`, lines 10-23), and registers every route (`setupRoutes`, lines 25-138). That single method is ~114 lines and contains HTTP parsing, validation, the payment rule, user creation, enrollment, the audit log, the financial report and the deletion.
Impact: Nothing can be tested in isolation, and any change touches every domain at once. The class has 6+ responsibilities.
Recommendation: Split it into config / database (connection + schema + seed) / models / controllers / routes / composition root (Playbook P-03 + P-04).

### #3 [CRITICAL] Admin and destructive endpoints without authentication (AP-04)
File: src/AppManager.js:80
File: src/AppManager.js:131-136
Description: `GET /api/admin/financial-report` returns revenue and student names for every course, and `DELETE /api/users/:id` runs `DELETE FROM users WHERE id = ?`. Neither checks identity or permission.
Impact: Any client can read financial data and student PII, and can delete any user.
Recommendation: Add an authorization middleware with `ADMIN_TOKEN` from config, the `X-Admin-Token` header, constant-time comparison, and fail-closed behavior (403 when no token is configured) (Playbook P-12).

### #4 [CRITICAL] Insecure password storage / hashing (AP-05)
File: src/utils.js:17-23
File: src/AppManager.js:68
File: src/AppManager.js:18
Description: `badCrypto(pwd)` concatenates the first 2 characters of `Buffer.from(pwd).toString('base64')` 10,000 times and returns `hash.substring(0, 10)`. That is reversible encoding, with no salt, and it depends only on the first 2 base64 characters (≈ the first byte or two of the password). The checkout falls back to a default password, `badCrypto(p || "123456")` (line 68). The seed stores the password in plain text: `'123'` (line 18).
Impact: Different passwords produce the same "hash" (any password starting with the same letter collides). A database leak exposes the credentials directly.
Recommendation: Use an adaptive hash with salt from the standard library (`crypto.scrypt` + `crypto.randomBytes` + `timingSafeEqual`) and remove the default password (Playbook P-05).

### #5 [CRITICAL] Sensitive data exposed in the log (AP-06)
File: src/AppManager.js:45
Description: `console.log(\`Processando cartão ${cc} na chave ${config.paymentGatewayKey}\`)` prints the full card number (PAN) and the payment gateway key.
Impact: Card data and the secret key leak to stdout and to log aggregators, which also violates PCI-DSS.
Recommendation: Never log the PAN or the key. At most log the last 4 digits, through a logger with levels (Playbook P-06 + P-19).

### #6 [HIGH] Business rules in the HTTP layer (Fat Route) (AP-07)
File: src/AppManager.js:28-78
File: src/AppManager.js:80-129
Description: The checkout handler decides the payment (`cc.startsWith("4") ? "PAID" : "DENIED"`, line 46), creates the user, creates the enrollment, payment and audit rows, and writes to the cache, all inside the route. The report handler computes `courseData.revenue += payment.amount` (lines 108-110) and assembles the response.
Impact: The rules are tied to Express, so they cannot be reused or tested without starting the server.
Recommendation: Move the flow to `CheckoutController` / `ReportController` and keep each route to request → controller → response (Playbook P-04).

### #7 [HIGH] Hard coupling without dependency injection (AP-08)
File: src/AppManager.js:7
File: src/AppManager.js:1-2
File: src/app.js:9-11
Description: The constructor instantiates `new sqlite3.Database(':memory:')` itself and requires the concrete `./utils` module (config, cache, hash). `app.js` depends on `new AppManager()` with no way to swap anything.
Impact: The DB and services cannot be replaced with fakes in tests, and changing the infrastructure means editing business rules.
Recommendation: Inject the DB connection and services through constructors, assembled in a single composition root (Playbook P-07).

### #8 [HIGH] Mutable global state (AP-09)
File: src/utils.js:9
File: src/utils.js:10
File: src/utils.js:12-15
Description: `let globalCache = {}` and `let totalRevenue = 0` live at module scope and are exported. `logAndCache` writes `globalCache[key] = data` on every checkout (AppManager.js:59) and never evicts anything.
Impact: Unbounded memory growth, state shared between requests and tests, and a `totalRevenue` value that is exported "by value" and never stays in sync.
Recommendation: Remove the global state. If a cache is needed, encapsulate it in an injected class (Playbook P-07 / P-08).

### #9 [HIGH] Multi-step write without a transaction / broken referential integrity (AP-10)
File: src/AppManager.js:69
File: src/AppManager.js:50-61
File: src/AppManager.js:133-135
Description: The checkout does `INSERT users` → `INSERT enrollments` → `INSERT payments` → `INSERT audit_logs` with no `BEGIN/COMMIT/ROLLBACK`. The deletion runs `DELETE FROM users WHERE id = ?` without touching `enrollments`/`payments`, and the response itself says so: "as matrículas e pagamentos ficaram sujos no banco" ("the enrollments and payments were left dirty in the database").
Impact: A failure halfway through leaves an enrollment without a payment, or a user without an enrollment. The deletion leaves orphan rows, which then show up as 'Unknown' in the report.
Recommendation: Put the checkout in a single transaction, and delete the dependents (payments → enrollments → user) in a transaction (Playbook P-09).

### #10 [HIGH] Callback hell / nested async flow (AP-11)
File: src/AppManager.js:26
File: src/AppManager.js:37-77
File: src/AppManager.js:83-128
Description: Callbacks are nested 5 levels deep in the checkout (`db.get` → `db.get` → `db.run` → `db.run` → `db.run`) and 4 levels in the report. The report uses manual counters (`coursesPending--`, `enrPending--; if (enrPending === 0) ...`, lines 86-121), and `const self = this` (line 26) works around `function(err)`.
Impact: The flow is hard to read, errors are silenced, and a response may be sent twice or never.
Recommendation: Promisify the driver (`util.promisify` / a small wrapper) and use `async/await` (Playbook P-10).

### #11 [MEDIUM] N+1 queries (AP-13)
File: src/AppManager.js:89-92
File: src/AppManager.js:102-106
Description: For each course, `SELECT * FROM enrollments WHERE course_id = ?`, and for each enrollment, `SELECT name, email FROM users WHERE id = ?` plus `SELECT amount, status FROM payments WHERE enrollment_id = ?`. That is 1 + C + 2·E queries in total.
Impact: Latency grows linearly with the number of enrollments.
Recommendation: Use a single query with `LEFT JOIN` across courses/enrollments/users/payments and group in memory (Playbook P-13).

### #12 [MEDIUM] Missing/inconsistent input validation (AP-14)
File: src/AppManager.js:29-35
File: src/AppManager.js:46
Description: Only presence is checked (`if (!u || !e || !cid || !cc)`). There are no type checks: `cc.startsWith("4")` runs inside a sqlite callback, so a `card` sent as a number throws an uncaught `TypeError` outside the Express stack, which crashes the process. `pwd` is optional and silently becomes "123456".
Impact: A single malformed request takes down the whole API, and users are created with a known password.
Recommendation: Use one validator for the checkout payload (string types, card made of digits, required password), returning 400 (Playbook P-14).

### #13 [MEDIUM] Duplicated code (AP-15)
File: src/AppManager.js:96-98
File: src/AppManager.js:119-121
Description: The block `report.push(courseData); coursesPending--; if (coursesPending === 0) res.json(report);` is repeated in two branches of the report.
Impact: The completion logic is spread out, which makes divergent changes and double responses easy.
Recommendation: Replace it with aggregation in the model / shared function, and remove the manual counter (Playbook P-15).

### #14 [MEDIUM] Error handling not centralized / errors swallowed (AP-16)
File: src/AppManager.js:38
File: src/AppManager.js:57-61
File: src/AppManager.js:92-93
File: src/AppManager.js:104-106
File: src/AppManager.js:133-135
Description: Line 38 turns any DB error into 404 "Curso não encontrado" ("course not found"). The audit_logs callback ignores `err` (57). The report ignores `err` in 3 queries. At 92-93, if the query fails, `enrollments.length` throws a TypeError and crashes the process. The DELETE ignores `err` and always replies 200. Error responses are plain text (`res.send("Erro DB")`) while successes are JSON, and there is no `(err, req, res, next)` middleware.
Impact: Errors are silent, status codes are wrong, the process can crash, and the error format is inconsistent.
Recommendation: Use domain exceptions (`NotFoundError`, `ValidationError`...) and one central error middleware that keeps the current bodies (Playbook P-11).

### #15 [LOW] Magic numbers / magic strings (AP-19)
File: src/AppManager.js:46
File: src/AppManager.js:48
File: src/AppManager.js:68
File: src/utils.js:6
Description: The payment rule is coded as a literal (`cc.startsWith("4")`). The status values `"PAID"`/`"DENIED"` are repeated (lines 46, 48, 108). The default password is `"123456"`, and the port `3000` is fixed.
Impact: Business rules are implicit and spread through the code.
Recommendation: Use named constants in the domain module (`PaymentStatus`, the approval rule), and take the port from config (Playbook P-17).

### #16 [LOW] Poor naming (AP-20)
File: src/AppManager.js:29-33
File: src/AppManager.js:4
File: src/utils.js:17
Description: Internal variables `u`, `e`, `p`, `cid`, `cc`, plus `c`/`enr` in the report. Names that misrepresent what they are: `AppManager` (God Class), `badCrypto`, and `utils` containing config. The contract fields (`usr`, `eml`, `pwd`, `c_id`, `card`) must stay as they are.
Impact: The code is hard to read, and the names hide responsibilities.
Recommendation: Rename the internal variables and modules only (Playbook P-18).

### #17 [LOW] Unused imports / dead code (AP-21)
File: src/AppManager.js:2
File: src/utils.js:10
File: src/utils.js:25
File: src/utils.js:2-3
File: src/utils.js:5
Description: `totalRevenue` is imported and never used. `globalCache` and `totalRevenue` are exported and never read anywhere else. `config.dbUser`, `config.dbPass` and `config.smtpUser` are never used (the DB is in-memory and there is no SMTP).
Impact: Noise, and secrets that look like they are needed when they are not.
Recommendation: Remove the dead exports, imports and config (Playbook P-18).

### #18 [LOW] `console.log` as the logger (AP-22)
File: src/utils.js:13
File: src/app.js:13
File: src/AppManager.js:45
Description: `console.log("[LOG] Salvando no cache...")`, the boot message, and the payment log (the last one is also #5).
Impact: No log levels and no way to filter or silence output per environment.
Recommendation: Use a small encapsulated logger with levels (Playbook P-19).

## Deprecated APIs
none detected for Node.js 22 + Express 4.22.1 + sqlite3 5.1.7 (no `new Buffer`, `url.parse`, `createCipher`, `req.param`, `app.del` or `body-parser`; the project already uses `express.json()` and `Buffer.from`). The nested-callback node-sqlite3 API is not deprecated but is obsolete; it is covered in #10.

## Notes
- All queries already use `?` placeholders, so there is no SQL Injection (AP-02 not reported).
- There is no debug flag or fake token (AP-12 not reported), and no email sending (AP-18 not reported).
- The DB is in-memory and seeded at boot, so the baseline will be captured with a fresh process.
- There is no `reports/` directory at the repo root, so the report is only printed.

